1
00:03:22,000 --> 00:03:28,000
Replace is a thriller, horror, science fiction film directed by Norbert Keil.

2
00:06:44,000 --> 00:06:50,000
Kira's skin starts to age rapidly, dry out and crumble away.

3
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:12,000
But then she discovers that she can replace her own skin with somebody else's.

4
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:34,000
Kira Mabon, played by Rebecca Forsythe.

5
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:56,000
Sophia Demeraux, played by Lucie Aron.

6
00:20:12,000 --> 00:20:18,000
Dr. Rafaela Crober, played by Barbara Crampton.

7
00:23:34,000 --> 00:23:40,000
Jonas Swierczynski, played by Sean Knopp.

8
00:26:56,000 --> 00:27:02,000
Dr. Czerny, played by Adnan Maral.

9
00:30:18,000 --> 00:30:24,000
Dr. Assistant, played by Agnes Kiyomi Decker.

10
00:33:40,000 --> 00:33:46,000
It (titled onscreen as It Chapter One) is a 2017 American supernatural horror film directed by
Andy Muschietti and written by Chase Palmer, Cary Fukunaga, and Gary Dauberman.

11
00:37:02,000 --> 00:37:08,000
It is the first of a two-part adaptation of the 1986 novel of the same name by Stephen King, as
well as the second adaptation following Tommy Lee Wallace's 1990 miniseries.

12
00:40:24,000 --> 00:40:30,000
Starring Jaeden Lieberher and Bill Skarsgård, the film was produced by New Line
Cinema, KatzSmith Productions, Lin Pictures, and Vertigo Entertainment.

13
00:43:46,000 --> 00:43:52,000
Set in the fictional town of Derry, Maine, the film tells the story of The Losers' Club, a group of seven outcast children who are terrorized by the eponymous being
which emerges from the sewer in the form of Pennywise the Dancing Clown (Skarsgård), and face their own personal demons as the monster torments them.

14
00:47:08,000 --> 00:47:14,000
Development began in March 2009 by Warner Bros., with David Kajganich planned
to direct, before being replaced by Fukunaga in June 2012.

15
00:50:30,000 --> 00:50:36,000
Fukunaga dropped out in May 2015 and was replaced by Muschietti in June.

16
00:53:52,000 --> 00:53:58,000
He drew inspiration from 1980s films including The Howling (1981), The Thing (1982), The Goonies (1985),
Stand by Me (1986) and Near Dark (1987), and cited the influence of Steven Spielberg.

17
00:57:14,000 --> 00:57:20,000
The film was moved to New Line Cinema division in May 2014.

18
01:00:36,000 --> 01:00:42,000
Principal photography began in Toronto on June 27,
2016, and ended on September 21, 2016.

19
01:03:58,000 --> 01:04:04,000
It was filmed on locations in the Greater Toronto Area,
including Port Hope, Oshawa, and Riverdale.

20
01:07:20,000 --> 01:07:26,000
Benjamin Wallfisch was hired in March 2017 to compose the musical score.

21
01:10:42,000 --> 01:10:48,000
It premiered at the TCL Chinese Theatre, Los Angeles, on September 5, 2017, and was
released in the United States on September 8, in 2D and IMAX formats.

22
01:14:04,000 --> 01:14:10,000
A critical and commercial success, the film set box office records and grossed over $704 million worldwide, becoming the
third-highest-grossing R-rated film at the time of its release, and grossed over $719 million through subsequent re-releases.

23
01:17:26,000 --> 01:17:32,000
Unadjusted for inflation, it became the
highest-grossing horror film of all time.

24
01:20:48,000 --> 01:20:54,000
The film received generally positive reviews, with critics praising the performances, direction,
cinematography and musical score, and was called one of the best Stephen King adaptations.

25
01:24:10,000 --> 01:24:16,000
It received numerous awards and nominations, including a nomination for the
Critics' Choice Movie Award for Best Sci-Fi/Horror Movie.

26
01:27:32,000 --> 01:27:38,000
It appeared on several critics' end-of-year lists.

27
01:30:54,000 --> 01:31:00,000
The second film, It Chapter Two, was released in 2019,
covering the remainder of the book.

28
01:34:16,000 --> 01:34:22,000
A prequel television series, It: Welcome to Derry, premiered on
HBO in 2025, with Skarsgård returning as Pennywise.

29
01:37:38,000 --> 01:37:44,000
The tone is dark and thought_provoking and intense.
